{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775332889-1w32aoA11KCg5qMX7qPM","role":"assistant","created":1775332906,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I need to extract the data for 1979 and 1984 from these tables. Let me parse out the monthly data.\n\nFrom 1985 document for 1984 (the 4th column group):\n- 1984 Treasury: Jan: 11.82, Feb: 12.00, Mar: 12.45, Apr: 12.65, May: 13.43, June: 13.54, July: 13.36, Aug: 12.71, Sept: 12.42, Oct: 12.04, Nov: 11.66, Dec: 11.64\n- 1984 New Aa corporate: Jan: 12.65, Feb: 12.80, Mar: 13.36, Apr: 13.64, May: 14.41, June: 14.49, July: 14.25, Aug: 13.54, Sept: 13.37, Oct: 13.02, Nov: 12.40, Dec: 12.47\n\nFrom 1980 document for 1979 (the 4th column group):\n- 1979 Treasury: Jan: 8.43, Feb: 8.43, Mar: 8.45, Apr: 8.44, May: 8.55, June: 8.32, July: 8.35, Aug: 8.42, Sept: 8.68, Oct: 9.44, Nov: 9.80, Dec: 9.58\n- 1979 New Aa corporate: Jan: 9.47, Feb: 9.52, Mar: 9.65, Apr: 9.69, May: 9.82, June: 9.51, July: 9.47, Aug: 9.57, Sept: 9.87, Oct: 11.17, Nov: 11.52, Dec: 11.30\n\nNow I need to calculate the Pearson correlation coefficient between Treasury and Corporate yields for each year.\n\n\nI'll use Python to compute the correlation. For 1979, the Treasury and corporate yields show strong alignment, with both starting at 8.43 and 9.47 respectively, and showing similar upward trends throughout the year. The 1984 data appears to have some inconsistencies in the corporate yields that I'll need to verify carefully.\n\nLooking at the actual numeric pattern, there's a clear positive correlation between Treasury and corporate yields across both years, with corporate rates consistently running about 1-1.5 percentage points above Treasury rates.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_4cd97ae1924c47e69bb10542","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\nimport math\n\n# 1979 data from treasury_bulletin_1980_03_page_88.txt (4th column group)\ntreasury_1979 = [8.43, 8.43, 8.45, 8.44, 8.55, 8.32, 8.35, 8.42, 8.68, 9.44, 9.80, 9.58]\ncorporate_1979 = [9.47, 9.52, 9.65, 9.69, 9.82, 9.51, 9.47, 9.57, 9.87, 11.17, 11.52, 11.30]\n\n# 1984 data from treasury_bulletin_1985_03_page_48.txt (4th column group)\ntreasury_1984 = [11.82, 12.00, 12.45, 12.65, 13.43, 13.54, 13.36, 12.71, 12.42, 12.04, 11.66, 11.64]\ncorporate_1984 = [12.65, 12.80, 13.36, 13.64, 14.41, 14.49, 14.25, 13.54, 13.37, 13.02, 12.40, 12.47]\n\ndef pearson_correlation(x, y):\n    n = len(x)\n    mean_x = sum(x) / n\n    mean_y = sum(y) / n\n    \n    numerator = sum((x[i] - mean_x) * (y[i] - mean_y) for i in range(n))\n    \n    sum_sq_x = sum((x[i] - mean_x) ** 2 for i in range(n))\n    sum_sq_y = sum((y[i] - mean_y) ** 2 for i in range(n))\n    \n    denominator = math.sqrt(sum_sq_x * sum_sq_y)\n    \n    if denominator == 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    return numerator / denominator\n\ncorr_1979 = pearson_correlation(treasury_1979, corporate_1979)\ncorr_1984 = pearson_correlation(treasury_1984, corporate_1984)\n\nprint(f\"1979 Pearson correlation: {corr_1979}\")\nprint(f\"1984 Pearson correlation: {corr_1984}\")\n\nabs_diff = abs(corr_1979 - corr_1984)\nprint(f\"Absolute difference: {abs_diff}\")\nprint(f\"Rounded to 4 decimal places: {round(abs_diff, 4)}\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
